description:
## Am I affected?

You are affected if your application uses `better-auth` earlier than 1.1.6 and sends email verification links.

You are affected if both of these are true:

- [ ] Your `better-auth` version is earlier than 1.1.6.
- [ ] Your application uses the email verification flow.

## Summary

Email verification links in Better Auth did not check where they sent users after verification. An attacker could create a verification link that sends users to a website the attacker controls. This type of flaw is called an open redirect.

## Impact

An attacker can create a verification link that redirects users to any untrusted website. The attacker can use this for phishing, by sending users to fake login or account pages. If users interact with those fake pages, their credentials may be stolen. The flaw can also harm user trust and the reputation of applications that use Better Auth.

## Patches

Better Auth 1.1.6 fixes this issue. Upgrade `better-auth` to 1.1.6 or later.

## Workarounds

Until you can upgrade, you can check redirect URLs yourself with a `before` hook:

```ts
const auth = betterAuth({
  hooks: {
    before: (ctx) => {
      if (ctx.path === "/verify-email") {
        const url = ctx.query.callbackURL;
        // Implement custom allowlist validation here
      }
    }
  }
});
```

Replace the comment with your own check against a list of allowed URLs. This hook only adds a layer of protection until you deploy the patched version.

## Technical details

The email verification endpoint (`/verify-email`) accepts a `callbackURL` parameter. After a successful verification, it redirects the user to that URL. Other flows check the origin (domain) of redirect URLs, but this check only ran for `POST` requests. Email verification uses a `GET` request, so the endpoint redirected users to external sites without any check.

Version 1.1.6 adds strict domain validation for the `callbackURL` parameter on `/verify-email` and on all other redirect endpoints that use `GET` requests.
